          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In great generality, a
            %em homotopy limit
            is a way of constructing an appropriate sorts of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} (weak) higher category
            and in general and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            theory in particular, using some
            %em presentation
            of that higher category by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} 1-categorical
            structure. The general study of such presentations is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            In classical homotopy theory, the presentation is given by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+in+a+quasi-category"} (∞,1)-categorical limits
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+homotopy+theory"} enriched homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+model+category"} enriched model category
            or an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+homotopical+category"} enriched homotopical category
            = succeed "-category\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            context the appropriate notion is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted
            homotopy limit, which may construct “weighted
            = succeed "-limits\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-category\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            = succeed "-category\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+enrichment"} homotopical enrichment
          %p
            Maybe the most commonly encountered setup for homotopy limits is that where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            in question is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
          %h2#Terminology Terminology
          %p
            In older literature, such as the original work of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BousfieldKan"} Bousfield–Kan
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\colon I\to sSet
            is defined as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(I/-, X),
          %p
            where the weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I/-
            sends
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\mapsto N(I/i)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            (for more see the section “
            %a{:href => "#ResolvedCoEnds"}> Resolved (co)ends
            ”
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield-Kan+map"} Bousfield-Kan map
          %p
            Such a functor must be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived
            in order to get the correct (homotopy invariant) notion, which in this case amounts to replacing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with an objectwise weakly equivalent diagram valued in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complexes"} Kan complexes
          %p At some point (when?) a shift in terminology happened, and in the modern parlance homotopy limits are commonly assumed to be derived.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            As for ordinary
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                with explicit constructions that satisfy a
                %em local
                universal property: the homotopy limit object “represents homotopy-coherent cones up to homotopy.”
            %li
              %p
                as
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
                that satisfy a
                %em global
                universal property: the homotopy limit
                %em functor
                is “universal among homotopical approximations to the strict limit functor.”
          %p One of the central theorems of the subject is that in good cases, the two give equivalent results; see below.
          %h3#GlobalDefinition Global definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be a (small)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            category. Make the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
            into a category with weak equivalences by taking the weak equivalences to be those
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            The ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            operations on
            = succeed "-diagrams" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoints
            of the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi const
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} const : C \to [D,C]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            along the unique functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !\colon D\to *
            to the terminal category.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi lim
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi Ran
                          %mo !
                    %mover
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi const
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                          %mi colim
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi Lan
                            %mo !
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D,C]
                  \\stackrel{\stackrel{\overset{colim = Lan_!}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{const}{\leftarrow}}}{\underset{lim = Ran_!}{\to}}
                  C
                  \\,.
          %p
            The (globally defined)
            %em homotopy limit
            and
            %em colimit
            are accordingly the right and left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
            along
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !\colon D\to *
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong homotopy limit
                of a functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
                is, if it exists, the image of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                under the right
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi lim
                        %mi D
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim_D : [D,C] \to C
                with respect to the given weak equivalences on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and the objectwise weak equivalences on
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi holim
                        %mi D
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℝ
                      %msub
                        %mi lim
                        %mi D
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi F
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      holim_D F := (\mathbb{R} lim_D)F
                      \\,.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong homotopy colimit
                of a functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
                is, if it exists, the image of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                under the left
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi colim
                        %mi D
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim_D : [D,C] \to C
                with respect to the given weak equivalences on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and the objectwise weak equivalences on
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mi D
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕃
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hocolim_D F := (\mathbb{L} colim_D)F
                  \\,.
          %p
            Alternative definitions can be formulated at the level of the homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^{-1} C
            one defines a localized version
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi I
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi W
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{\Delta}^I : W^{-1} C\to W_I^{-1} C^I
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi I
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^I : C\to C^I
            and define the homotopy limits and colimits as the adjoints of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{\Delta}^I
            (at least at the points where the adjoints are defined). Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi I
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi Mor
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_I\subset Mor(C^I)
            are the morphisms of diagrams whose all components are in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W\subset Mor(C)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Delta^I,\bar{\Delta}^I)
            is a morphism of localizers (and in particular that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{\Delta}^I
            with the identity 2-cell is a Kan extension (simultaneously left and right)).
          %p
            In the enriched case, this must be suitably modified to deal with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limits
            as well as enrichment of both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            In particular, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is equipped with the extra structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is an (small)
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            we may also hope to equip the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched functor
            category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
            with the structure of a simplicial model category. There are two different “canonical” such structures:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em projective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi D
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi proj
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{proj}
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em injective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi inj
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{inj}
          %p
            (both of which have the same objectwise weak equivalences and are in fact
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi proj
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mi const
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi colim
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D,C]_{proj}
                  \\stackrel{\overset{colim}{\longrightarrow}}{\underset{const}{\longleftarrow}}
                  C
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi inj
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi lim
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mi const
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D,C]_{inj}
                  \\stackrel{\overset{const}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{lim}{\longrightarrow}}
                  C
                  \\,.
          %p
            (All proofs and other technical details on this are at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
          %p
            These present directly the corresponding
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Func
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ←
                    %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\infty,1)Func(D,C^\circ)
                  \\stackrel{\longrightarrow}{\stackrel{\leftarrow}{\longrightarrow}}
                  C^\circ
          %p by precomposition with a cofibrant replacement functor (for the colimit) and a fibrant replacement functor (for the limit).
          %h3#local_definition Local definition
          %p
            The local definition requires making precise the notion of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              commutative cone
            on a diagram.
          %p
            For the case of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SimpSet"} SimpSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enrichment
            one elegant way to do so is in terms of suitable
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
            on a diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : K \to C
            in an
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Simp\Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \Rightarrow C(c,F(-)) : K \to \Simp\Set
            where the
            %em weight
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is not constant on the point, as for ordinary limits, but is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo :
                    %mi k
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W : k \mapsto N(K/k)
          %p
            The same idea works if we are enriched over a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            that is not
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} SimpSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \SimpSet
            but is itself enriched over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Simp\Set
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Simp\Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            need not be enriched over anything, it is always “almost” enriched over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Simp
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Simp\Set
            %em framings
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %em resolutions
          %p
            Following the reasoning described in Example 1 of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            one then defines the homotopy limit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            of a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: K \to C
            to be a representing object for such homotopy cones, in the sense that we have a (weak) equivalence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi HoCones
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(X,L) \simeq HoCones(X,F)
          %p
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-objects"} hom-objects
            (spaces or simplicial sets in the classical context; enriched hom-objects in the enriched context).
          %h3#global_versus_local Global versus local
          %p
            The global definition is formulated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalences
            only, while the local definition is formulated in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopies
            only. However, in practical cases, derived functors exist because their input objects (in this case, the diagram
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %em homotopy
            equivalences. The derived functor of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim
            at the input object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is then computed by applying the ordinary functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi lim
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim
            to a good replacement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R F
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            It then turns out that the “good” (precisely, “fibrant”) replacement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R F
            “builds in” precisely the right homotopies so that an ordinary cone on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R F
            is the same as a homotopy-commutative cone on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi lim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim (R F)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p Formal versions of this argument can be found in many places. Perhaps the original statement can be found in XI.8.1 of:
          %ul
            %li
              A. K. Bousfield and D. M. Kan,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Homotopy limits, completions, and localizations
          %p (As was often the case with Kan’s papers at that time, there are some details omitted from that treatment, but most are, as he claimed, quite easy to complete.) For another approach in an algebraic context, there is a description in Illusie’s thesis.
          %p An abstract version in modern language, with proof, can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              Michael Shulman,
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610194"} Homotopy limits and colimits and enriched homotopy theory
          %h4#strictness Strictness
          %p
            Another notable difference between the local and global definitions is that the global definition can only ever define the homotopy limit up to weak equivalence (isomorphism in the homotopy category), while in the local definition we
            %em could
            require, if we wanted to, an actual isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi HoCones
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(X,L) \cong HoCones(X,F)
          %p
            of hom-objects, rather than merely a weak equivalence. By analogy with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-limit"} strict 2-limits
            = succeed "." do
              %strong strict homotopy limit
          %p
            Frequently a strict homotopy limit does in fact exist, and can be constructed as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
            in the ordinary (enriched) category in question. In such cases, the strict homotopy limit may be easier to compute with than an arbitrary homotopy limit merely known to have the up-to-weak-equivalence universal property. Thus, sometimes when people say
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            homotopy limit they refer mean a
            %em strict
            homotopy limit.
          %p When a strict homotopy limit exists, an arbitrary homotopy limit may be defined as an object which is (weakly) equivalent to the strict homotopy limit.
          %h3#Derivators Derivators
          %p
            It is noteworthy that the homotopy limit and colimit in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            are drastically different from the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            and colimit in the corresponding
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            This (obvious) observation is a very old one and can be seen to be one of the driving forces behind the insight that just working with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
            misses crucial information, something which today is understood as the statement that a homotopy category is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorification
            of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %p
            While the full theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            is one way to impose the correct notion of higher categorical limits, there are other ways to deal with issue. Due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Alexander Grothendieck
            is the technique of using
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"}> derivator
              s
            for keeping track of homotopy limits.
          %p
            Roughly, the idea of a derivator is that while the single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C)
            of a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with weak equivalences is not sufficient information, the homotopy limit of a diagram in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
            %em is
            encoded in the homotopy category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho([D,C])
            of that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            (this is after all the domain of the plain 1-categorical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of the limit functor). Accordingly, what is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
            of a category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a collection of
            %em all
            the homotopy categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho([D,C])
            of
            %em all
            the possible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            runs over all small categories. See there for more details.
          %h2#computational_methods Computational methods
          %p Above we defined homotopy (co)limits in general. There are various more specific formulas and algorithms for computing homotopy (co)limits. Here we discuss some of these
          %h3#ordinary_colimits_on_resolved_diagrams Ordinary (co)limits on resolved diagrams
          %p
            The direct prescription for computing the value of a right or left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            is by evaluating the original functor on a fibrant or cofibrant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of the given object.
          %p For the derived functors of limit and colimit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %mi lim
                            %mo ⟶
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi const
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %munder
                          %mi lim
                          %mo ⟵
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D,C] \stackrel{\overset{\lim_\longleftarrow}{\longrightarrow}}{\stackrel{\overset{const}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{\lim_\longrightarrow}{\longrightarrow}}}
                  C
          %p let for instance
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi proj
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{proj} : [D,C] \to [D,C]
            be a cofibrant replacement for the projective
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            the diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi proj
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{proj} F
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projectively+cofibrant+diagram"} projectively cofibrant diagram
            (see there for more details). Then the homotopy colimit is presented by the ordinary colimit on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi proj
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{proj} F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕃
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≃
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} limt
                    %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi proj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\mathbb{L}\underset{\to}{\lim}) F \simeq \underset{\to}{\limt} Q_{proj}(F)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is sometimes called the
            %strong Quillen formula
            for computing homotopy colimits. Analogously with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi inj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{inj}
            a fibrant replacement functor for the injective model structure, we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi holim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi lim
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi inj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  holim F \simeq lim P_{inj}(F)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Often, however, it is inconvenient to produce a resolution of a diagram. Because often all the work is in finding the resolution, while it is easy to evaluate the original functor on it. Therefore one wants ways to slightly change the setup of the problem such that the computation of the resolutions becomes more systematic. One such way is the use of derived (co)ends, discussed
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "ResolvedCoEnds"} below
          %h3#ResolvedCoEnds Resolved (co)ends
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi proj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q'_{proj}
            a cofibrant replacement functor for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi sSet
                    %mi Quillen
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi proj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D^{op}, sSet_{Quillen}]_{proj}
            (notice the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi inj
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{inj}
            one for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi inj
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{inj}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * \in [D^op,sSet]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaf"} simplicial presheaf
            constant on the terminal simplicial set and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi proj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q'_{proj}(')
            its projective cofibrant replacement.
          %p
            Then we also have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            expression
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hocolim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi D
                  %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi proj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi inj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hocolim F \simeq \int^{D}
                  Q'_{proj}(*) \cdot Q_{inj}(F)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where in the integrand we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensoring
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            happens to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+model+category"} Reedy model category
            we can equivalently use in this expression also the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+model+structure"} Reedy model structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi Reedy
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{Reedy}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi Reedy
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D^{op}, C]_{Reedy}
            and obtain the homotopy colimit as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hocolim
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi D
                  %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mo ′
                    %mi Reedy
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi Reedy
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hocolim F \simeq \int^{D}
                  Q'_{Reedy}(*) \cdot Q_{Reedy}(F)
                  \\,,
          %p
            This formula is sometimes called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield--Kan+formula"} Bousfield–Kan formula
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
            and this formula for the plain homotopy colimit can be understood the left derived
            %em weighted
            colimit which trivial weight (the underived weight is trivial, but becomes non-trivial after derivation – this extra complexity helps reduce the complexity for the replacement for the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            itself).
          %p
            In detail, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+model+category"} monoidal model category
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+model+category"} enriched model category
            (for instance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi sSet
                    %mi Quillen
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = sSet_{Quillen}
            as in the above discussion). Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be a small
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weight
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W : D^{op} \to V
          %p
            we have a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %munderover
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mrow
                        %mi const
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi W
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi D
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi D
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [D,C]
                  \\underoverset
                  {\underset{const(-)^{W}}{\longleftarrow}}
                  {\overset{W \otimes_D (-)}{\longrightarrow}}
                  {\;\;\;\bot\;\;\;}
                  D
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            is the weighted colimit given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi D
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi D
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  W \otimes_D F = \int^{d \in D} W(d) \otimes F(d)
          %p
            (with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensoring
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in the integran) and where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            is formed using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power"} cotensor
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            The crux now is that as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            varies, the left adjoint here is a left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+bifunctor"} Quillen bifunctor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int (-)\cdot (-)
                  \:
                  [D^{op}, V] \times [D,C]
                  \\to
                  C
                  \\,.
          %p
            For details on this see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+bifunctor"} Quillen bifunctor
            or around page 9 of (
            %a{:href => "#Gambino10"}> Gambino 10
            ).
          %p
            From the fact that this is a Quillen bifunctor and using the observation that for the
            %em trivial
            weight
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo =
                  %mi const
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W = const 1
            the weighted colimit reduces to an ordinary colimit, follows the above Bousfield-Kan-type formula for the homotopy colimit.
          %h3#BarConstruction Bar constructions
          %p
            A general way of obtaining resolutions that compute homotopy (co)limits involves
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+construction"} bar construction
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield--Kan+formula"} Bousfield–Kan formula
          %h2#homotopy_limits_versus_higher_categorical_limits Homotopy limits versus higher categorical limits
          %p
            As a special case of enriched homotopy theory, we may consider model categories or homotopical categories that are enriched over a notion of
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            to also be of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,r)+1 = (n+1,r+1)
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty+1=\infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"}> simplicial set
                s are models for
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoid"} groupoids
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,0)
                \-categories), so
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model categories
                are presentations for
                = succeed "-categories." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                = succeed "-categories," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                A
                = succeed "-enriched" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+2-category"} model 2-category
                or
                %span.newWikiWord
                  homotopical 2-category
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/homotopical+2-category"} ?
                is a presentation of a weak
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
                (or
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %li
              %p
                A
                = succeed ")-enriched" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mi Cat
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi Gray
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2Cat,\otimes_{Gray}
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-category"} Gray-category
                %span.newWikiWord
                  model Gray-category
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/model+Gray-category"} ?
                or
                %span.newWikiWord
                  homotopical Gray-category
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/homotopical+Gray-category"} ?
                is a presentation of a weak
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
                (or
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a category enriched over
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            %em be
            an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be a morphism that is an
            = succeed "-category-theoretic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            %em natural
            or
            %em trivial
            homotopical structure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2)
            it can be made into a model structure, also called
            %em natural
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em trivial
          %p
            Since higher categorical limits are generally defined as representing objects for cones that commute up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has a natural homotopical structure, locally-defined homotopy limits and
            = succeed "-limits" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
            this is trivial. For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
            it is proven in (
            %a{:href => "#Gambino10"}> Gambino 10
            ) (particularly section 6). For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=(\infty,1)
            it is proven in (among other places) Lurie’s book, section 4.2.4. The case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=3
            ought to be approachable in theory, but doesn’t seem to have been done (probably partly because the general theory of 3-limits is fairly nonexistent).
          %p
            On the other hand, we may also consider a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            enriched over
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em larger
            class of weak equivalences than just the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            presents an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category\u201D)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with this homotopical structure should then present
            = succeed "-limits" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=(\infty,1)
            this is also essentially in Lurie’s book; for other values of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            it may not be in the literature.
          %h2#homotopy_limits_versus_limits_in_the_homotopy_category Homotopy limits versus limits in the homotopy category
          %p
            It is important to note that homotopy limits and limits in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            are, in general, incomparable. A homotopy limit need not be a limit in the homotopy category, while a limit in the homotopy category need not be a homotopy limit.
          %p
            It is generally true that homotopy
            %em products
            (and coproducts) are also products and coproducts in the homotopy category. Some other homotopy limits induce the corresponding notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+limit"} weak limit
            in the homotopy category; for instance, homotopy pullbacks become weak pullbacks in the homotopy category. However, even this is not true for all types of homotopy limit.
          %p
            On the other hand, homotopy categories do not
            %em usually
            have many limits and colimits at all (aside from products and coproducts). An explicit proof that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(Cat)
            does not have pullbacks can be found
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Cat%29#OtherLimits"} here
            %em does
            happen to have limits and colimits, these need not be the same as homotopy limits.
          %p
            For instance, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is quasi-isomorphic to its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            is equivalent to the category of graded
            = succeed "-vector-spaces." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable (infinity,1)-category
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#WeightedColimitFormula General weighted colimit formula for homotopy colimits
          %p Let
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                be a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            %li
              %p
                let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                be a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
              %p
                (possibly an ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
                regarded as a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
                in the tautological way)
            %li
              %p
                and let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
                be an
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched functor
          %p
            There is a general formula for the homotopy colimit over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            in terms of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+colimit"} weighted colimit
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h4#ingredients Ingredients
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+simplicial+model+category"} combinatorial simplicial model category
            as above and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicially+enriched+category"} simplicially enriched category
            there is the projective and the injective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+model+structure+on+functors"} global model structure on functors
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor+category"} enriched functor category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In the projective model structure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi proj
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{proj}
                the fibrations and the local equivalences are objectwise those of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                In the injective model structure
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi inj
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{inj}
                the cofibrations and the local equivalences are objectwise those of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Each of these is itself a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+simplicial+model+category"} combinatorial simplicial model category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
            applies in these using which one obtains cofibrant replacement functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi proj
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q_{proj} : [D, C] \to [D, C]
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi inj
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q_{inj} : [D, C] \to [D, C]
                  \\,.
          %p
            That
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
            implies in particular that it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensored
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            and that the tensoring functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\otimes : C \times sSet \to C
          %p
            is a left
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+bifunctor"} Quillen bifunctor
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensor
            in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi sSet
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi proj
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi inj
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int^D (-) \otimes (-) : [D^{op},sSet]_{proj} \times [D,C]_{inj} \to C
          %p and in the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi sSet
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi inj
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi proj
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\int^D (-) \otimes (-) : [D^{op},sSet]_{inj} \times [D,C]_{proj} \to C
          %p
            both themselves left
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+bifunctor"} Quillen bifunctor
          %p Write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  {*} : D^{op} \to sSet
          %p
            for the functor that sends everything to the identity on the singleton set. This is the tensor unit in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D^{op},sSet]
          %h4#general_formula General formula
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              With the above assumptions and ingredients, the homotopy colimit over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
              is given either by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi D
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi proj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi inj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    hocolim F = \int^D Q_{proj}({*}) \otimes Q_{inj}(F)
            %p or by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mi F
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi D
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi inj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi proj
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    hocolim F = \int^D Q_{inj}({*}) \otimes Q_{proj}(F)
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By the fact that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensor
              appearing here is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+bifunctor"} Quillen bifunctor
            %p
              This is disucssed for instance in section 4 of (
              %a{:href => "#Gambino10"}> Gambino 10
              ).
          %h4#examples_2 Examples
          %h5#homotopy_colimits_of_simplicial_diagrams Homotopy colimits of simplicial diagrams
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \Delta^{op}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A cofibrant replacement of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*}
              in the projective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+model+structure+on+functors"} global model structure on functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta, sSet]
              is the the
              = succeed "-functor" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fat+simplex"} fat simplex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undercategory"} undercategory
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^{op}
              under
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo :
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSet
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N(-/\Delta^{op})^{op} : \Delta \to sSet
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p For instance prop 14.8.8 in
            %ul
              %li
                Hirschhorn,
                %em Model categories and their localization
          %p
            Notice that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \Delta^{op} \to C
            takes values in cofibrant objects of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi inj
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta^{op},C]_{inj}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is necessary and it therefore follows with the general formula and the above proposition that the homotopy colimit over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is given by the formulas
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hocolim
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Δ
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi Δ
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hocolim F = \int^{[n] \in \Delta} N([n]/\Delta)^{op} \otimes F(n)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is famously the formula introduced and used by Bousfield and Kan (but there originally missing the necessary condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be objectwise cofibrant). See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield-Kan+map"} Bousfield-Kan map
          %h4#homotopy_pushouts Homotopy pushouts
          %p
            Let in the above general formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ←
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \{a \leftarrow c \to b\}
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking"} walking
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span"} span
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %strong homotopy pushouts
          %p In this simple case, we have the following simple observation:
          .num_lemma
            %h6#observation Observation
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              as above, the terminal functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*} : D \to sSet
              is already cofibrant in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi D
                      %mo ,
                      %mi sSet
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi inj
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,sSet]_{inj}
          %p Moreover
          .num_lemma
            %h6#observation_2 Observation
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              as above, a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
              is cofibrant in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi proj
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D,C]_{proj}
              if
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  it sends both morphisms
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo →
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \to a
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo →
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \to b
                  to cofibrations
              %li
                %p
                  it sends
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                  (and hence also
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                  and
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            Since a coend
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∫
                  %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int {* } \otimes F
            over a tensor product where the first factor in the integrand in the tensor unit is just an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            over the remaining
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is of the form of the above observation, then the ordinary colimit over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            already computes the homotopy pushout:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hocolim
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hocolim F = \lim_\to F
                  \\,.
          %p The dual version of this statement (for homotopy limits and homotopy pullbacks) is discussed in more detail in the examples below.
          %h3#HomotopyPullbacks Homotopy pullbacks
          %p
            Here we consider special cases of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            in more detail.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ←
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = \{ 1\to 0 \leftarrow 2\}
            be the pullback
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to C
            is such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ←
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F(1) \to F(0) \leftarrow F(2)
          %p satisfies
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(i)
              is fibrant for all
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %li
              and either
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1) \to F(0)
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(2) \to F(0)
              is a fibration;
          %p then
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi holim
                      %mi D
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} holim_D F
              exists;
            %li
              and is weakly equivalent to the ordinary limit
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi holim
                        %mi D
                      %mi F
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi lim
                        %mi D
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} holim_D F \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} lim_D F
          %p
            Conversely this means that on an arbitrary pullback diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi holim
                    %mi D
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} holim_D F
            can be computed by finding a natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \Rightarrow R F
            whose component morphisms are weak equivalences and such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R F
            satisfies the above conditions.
          %h4#based_loop_objects Based loop objects
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed object
            with point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pt
                        %mi B
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt \stackrel{pt_B}{\longrightarrow} B
            the homotopy pullback of the point along itself is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi pt
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Omega_{pt} &amp;\to&amp; {*}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  {*} &amp;\to&amp; B
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p i.e.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi holim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pt
                        %mi B
                  %mi B
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi pt
                        %mi B
                  %mi pt
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi pt
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  holim(  pt \stackrel{pt_B}{\longrightarrow}  B \stackrel{pt_B}{\leftarrow} pt)
                  \\;\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}\;
                  \\Omega_{pt} B
                  \\,.
          %p
            One way to compute this using the above prescription by noticing that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi pt
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}_{pt} B
            provides a fibrant replacement of the pullback diagram in that we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Id
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                            %mi pt
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  pt &amp;\to&amp; B &amp;\leftarrow&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\simeq} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{Id} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{Id}
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{E}_{pt}B &amp;\to&amp; B &amp;\leftarrow&amp; pt
                  }
          %p with all vertical morphisms weak equivalences and with the left bottom horizontal morphism a fibration.
          %p More on that in the further examples below.
          %h4#fibration_sequences Fibration sequences
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*} \to C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  A &amp;\to&amp; {*}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  B &amp;\to&amp; C
                  }
          %p
            the sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to B \to C
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration+sequence"} fibration sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the
            %em homotopy kernel
            or
            %em homotopy fiber
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \to C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Omega C &amp;\to&amp; A &amp;\to&amp; {*}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  {*} &amp;\to&amp; B &amp;\to&amp; C
                  }
                  \\,.
          %h4#GroupDeloop Homotopy pullback of a point over a group / universal bundles
          %p As a special case of the above general example we get the following.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            equipped with the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            regarded as a discrete monoidal groupoid (elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            are the objects of the groupoids and all morphisms are identities) and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            for the corresponding one-object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            (single object, one morphism per element of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
            for the terminal groupoid (one object, no nontrivial morphism). Notice that there is a unique functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pt
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt \to \mathbf{B}G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi holim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi pt
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi pt
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  holim
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  pt &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                  G
                  \\,.
          %p
            To see this, we compute using the above prescription by finding a weakly equivalent pullback diagram such that one of its morphisms is a fibration. This is achieved in particular by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi pt
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt \stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow} \mathbf{E}G \to\gt \mathbf{B}G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G//G
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            acting on itself by multiplication from one side. So we have a weak equivalence of pullback diagrams
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo ≃
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo =
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo =
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi E
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  pt &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G &amp;\leftarrow&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\simeq} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^= &amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^=
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{E}G &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G &amp;\leftarrow&amp; pt
                  }
          %p
            and the homotopy limit in question; Is weakly equivalent to the ordinary limit over the lower diagram. That is directly seen to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Obj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Obj
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(Obj(\mathbf{E}G)) = Disc(Obj(G//G)) = Disc(G)
            which we just write as
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi holim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi pt
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi pt
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi lim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi pt
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                            %mi G
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  holim
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  pt &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                  lim
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; pt
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{E}G &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \= G
                  \\,.
          %p
            This example is important in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoidification"} groupoidification
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho:\mathbf{B}G\to {Top}
            is the datum of a toplogical space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            equipped with an action of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi colim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} colim(\rho)=X/G
            whereas
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi G
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hocolim(\rho)=\mathbf{E}G\times_G X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
          %h4#DeloopedSubgroupOverGroup Homotopy pullback of a subgroup over a group
          %p
            The above example generalizes straightforwardly to the case where the trivial inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt \to \mathbf{B}G
            is replaced by any inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                  %mo ↪
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}H \hookrightarrow \mathbf{B}G
            of any subgroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            pretty much literally by replacing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi pt
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} pt
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}H
            throughout.
          %p One finds
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi holim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi H
                  %mo \
                  %mo \
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  holim\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}H
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}H &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}  H \backslash\backslash G//H
          %p
            where on the right we have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ×
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \times H
            acting on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            by multiplication from the left (first factor) and the right (second factor). (See for instance at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hecke+category"} Hecke category
            for an application.)
          %p
            To see this, we again build a fibrant replacement of the pullback diagram. Following the constructions at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            consider first the groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi H
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{B}H}G
            given by the pullback diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                            %mrow
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi B
                              %mi H
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi H
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi I
                          %mo ,
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{B}H}G &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}H
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  [I, \mathbf{B}G] &amp;\stackrel{d_0}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{d_1}
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            As at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            one proves that the left vertical morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi H
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{B}H}G \to \mathbf{B}G
            is a fibration.
          %p
            Now, notice (which was implicit in the above example) that since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,\mathbf{B}G]
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+object"} path object
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}G \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}^\sigma  [I, \mathbf{B}G]
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 0
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,\mathbf{B}G] \stackrel{d_0}{\to} \mathbf{B}G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi σ
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi H
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}H \stackrel{\sigma}{\to} \mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{B}H}G
            making the obvious diagram commute. Now, the latter morphism, being the pullback of an acyclic fibration is an acyclic fibration, so its right inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            is a weak equivalence. This way we obtain the morphism of pullback diagrams
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi H
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi H
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mo ≃
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi σ
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Id
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Id
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                            %mrow
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi B
                              %mi H
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                          %mo &gt;
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{B}H &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G &amp;\leftarrow&amp; \mathbf{B}H
                  \\\
                  {}^\simeq\downarrow^{\sigma} &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{Id}
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{Id}
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{B}H}G &amp;\to \gt&amp; \mathbf{B}G &amp;\leftarrow&amp; \mathbf{B}H
                  }
          %p which is objectwise a weak equivalence and such that the horizontal morphism on the bottom left is a fibration. By the above statement the ordinary limit of the lower horizontal diagram is weakly equivalent to the homotopy limit we are looking for. But this is manifestly the desired action groupoid:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi holim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi lim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                %mi E
                              %mrow
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %mi H
                            %mi G
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi lim
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi I
                            %mo ,
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi d
                                  %mn 0
                                %mo ×
                                %msub
                                  %mi d
                                  %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                            %mo ×
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi H
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                            %mo ×
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mi H
                  %mo \
                  %mo \
                  %mi G
