                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  holim\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}H
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}H &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                  \\lim\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}H
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{E}_{\mathbf{B}H} G &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\simeq
                  \\lim\left(
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; [I,\mathbf{B}G]
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{d_0 \times d_1}
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}H \times \mathbf{B}H
                  \&amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G \times \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \=
                  H \backslash\backslash G//H
                  \\,.
          %p
            This example, too, is important at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+function+theory"} geometric function theory
          %h4#homotopy_span_traces Homotopy span traces
          %ul
            %li
              see the homotopy span traces discussed at
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/span+trace"} span trace
              for more examples of homotopy pullbacks
          %h3#OverSimplicialDiagrams Homotopy colimits of simplicial diagrams
          %p
            Recall that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            of its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+elements"} elements
            (
            %a{:href => "simplicial+set#SimplicialSetIsColimitOfItsSimplices"}> this Prop.
            ). The following says that this is in fact a homotopy colimit, up to equivalence.
          #SimplicialSetIsHomotopyColimitOverItself.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
                of its cells)
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
              over its cells.
            %p
              Precisely: for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Set
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde X
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\Delta^{op}
                    \\xrightarrow{\;}
                    Set
                    \\xhookrightarrow{\;}
                    sSet
            %p
              be the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+set"} bisimplicial set
              which in degree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              is the the constant simplicial set on the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_k
              of
              = succeed "-simplices." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %p
              For the standard homotopical structure on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi sSet
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet^{\Delta^{op}}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
              is equivalent to the original
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    hocolim \tilde X \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} X
            %p
              in the standard
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Use the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+model+structure"} Reedy model structure
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Quillen
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi Reedy
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta^{op}, sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Δ
                    %mi Q
                    %msub
                      %mo ′
                      %mi Reedy
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi Reedy
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\int^{[k] \in \Delta} Q'_{Reedy}(*) \times Q_{Reedy}(\tilde X)
                    \\,,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %msub
                      %mo ′
                      %mi Reedy
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q'_{Reedy}(\cdots)
              is a cofibrant resolution in the Reedy model structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi Reedy
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta,sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi Reedy
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_{Reedy}(...)
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi op
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Quillen
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi Reedy
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta^{op}, sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Reedy+model+structure#SimplexCategory"} Reedy model structure – simplex category
              we have that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
                  is always cofibrant in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msup
                            %mi Δ
                            %mi op
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi sSet
                            %mi Quillen
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mi Reedy
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta^{op},sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
              %li
                %p
                  a cofibrant resolution of the point in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi sSet
                          %mi Quillen
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi Reedy
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta, sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
                  is given by
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo :
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo →
                          %mi sSet
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[-] : \Delta \to sSet
            %p It follows that the hocolim is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Δ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\int^{[k] \in \Delta} \Delta[k] \times X_k
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    X
                    \\,.
            %p
              which is the expression of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is an ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              of its elements (
              %a{:href => "simplicial+set#SimplicialSetIsColimitOfItsSimplices"}> this Prop.
              , the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Yoneda+lemma"}> co-Yoneda lemma
              ”).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              More generally with this kind of argument it follows that generally the homotopy colimit over a simplicial diagram of simplicial sets is represented by the
              %a{:href => "bisimplicial+set#Diagonal"} diagonal simplicial set
              of the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimplicial+set"} bisimplicial set
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              This kind of argument has many immediate generalizations. For instance for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi inj
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = [K^{op}, sSet_{Quillen}]_{inj}
              the injective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
              over any small category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization+of+model+categories"} Bousfield localizations
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaf"} simplicial presheaf
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the hocolim over its simplicial diagram of component presheaves.
          %p
            For the following write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Delta} : \Delta \to sSet
            for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fat+simplex"} fat simplex
          %p
          .num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p The fat simplex is Reedy cofibrant.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              By the discussion at
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Quillen
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi proj
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta, sSet_{Quillen}]_{proj}
              %a{:href => "#Properties"} general properties
              of Reedy model structures, the identity functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi proj
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi Reedy
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta, sSet_{Quillen}]_{proj} \to [\Delta, sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} left Quillen functor
          .num_prop
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in [\Delta^{op}, C]
              a Reedy cofibrant object, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield-Kan+map"} Bousfield-Kan map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\int^{[n]} \mathbf{\Delta}[n] \cdot X_n
                    \\to
                    \\int^{[n]} \Delta[n] \cdot X_n
            %p
              is a weak equivalence in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .proof
            %h6#proof_5 Proof
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensor
              is a left
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+bifunctor"} Quillen bifunctor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi sSet
                      %mi Quillen
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi Reedy
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi Reedy
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\int (-)\cdot (-) : [\Delta,sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy} \times
                    [\Delta^{op}, C]_{Reedy}
            %p
              (as discussed there). Therefore with its second argument fixed and cofibrant it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} left Quillen functor
              in the remaining argument. As such, it preserves weak equivalences between cofibrant objects (by the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+lemma"} factorization lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Delta}[n]
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta[-]
              are indeed cofibrant in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Quillen
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mi Reedy
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\Delta,sSet_{Quillen}]_{Reedy}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Delta}[-] \to \Delta[-]
              is objectwise a weak equivalence in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi sSet
                        %mi Quillen
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet_{Quillen}
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi f
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : \Delta_f  \hookrightarrow \Delta
              be the inclusion into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
              of all the monomorphisms (all the face maps).
            %p
              This inclusion is a homotopy-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+functor"}> initial functor
              \. As a consequence, homotopy colimits of shape
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              can equivalently be computed after their restriction to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_f
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi f
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi i
                          %mi op
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    hocolim(  \Delta^{op} \stackrel{F}{\to} C)
                    \\simeq
                    hocolim( \Delta_f^{op} \stackrel{i^{op}}{\to} \Delta^{op} \stackrel{F}{\to} C)
                    \\,.
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Dugger"} Dugger, example 18.2
          %h3#homotopy_colimits_of_diagrams_of_spaces Homotopy colimits of diagrams of spaces
          %h4#by_geometric_realization By geometric realization
          %p The following is sometimes in the literature taken as the definition of homotopy colimits of diagrams of spaces. It is one of the earliest formulas for there.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %p
              Define a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+topological+space"} simplicial topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF
              by setting
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sF
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↦
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ←
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ←
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo ←
                        %msub
                          %mi d
                          %mi n
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    sF : [n] \mapsto \coprod_{d_0 \leftarrow d_1 \leftarrow \cdots \leftarrow d_n} F(d_n)
            %p
              and using the obvious face and degeneracy maps: face maps act by mapping components of the
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(d_n) \to F(d_{n-1})
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : D \to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is equivalent to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces"} geometric realization of simplicial topological spaces
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s F
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mi F
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi sF
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    hocolim F \simeq \vert sF \vert
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is an application of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield--Kan+formula"} Bousfield–Kan formula
          %p
            See for instance
            %a{:href => "#Dugger"} Dugger, part 1
            for an exposition.
          %h4#higher_homotopy_van_kampen_theorem Higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
              for its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
              and let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\chi : C \to Op(C)
            %p
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              out of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                for each point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_x
                of objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi(x)
                contains
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weakly
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            %p Then:
            %p
              the canonical morphism in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              out of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo →
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi χ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {\lim_\to} Sing \circ \chi \to Sing(X)
            %p
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing(X)
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hocolim
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi χ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hocolim Sing \circ \chi
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
            for details.
          %h3#SequentialHocolims Sequential homotopy (co)limits
          %h4#CotowerColimits General
          %p For
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D = \{0 \to 1 \to 2 \to \cdots\}
          %p
            the cotower category, a colimit of shape
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+colimit"} sequential colimit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
            it is easy to characterize the cofibrant objects in the projective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+functors"} model structure on functors
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi D
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi proj
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [D, C]_{proj}
          %p
            So given a cotower with such a property, its homotopy colimit is just the ordinary sequential colimit in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p Dually for sequential limits of a tower diagram.
          %p
            A standard application for this is for instance the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi n
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U = \underset{\underset{n}{\longrightarrow}}{\lim} B U(n)
            for reduced
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+unitary+group"} stable unitary group
            for more.
          %h4#_and_milnor_sequences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi lim
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lim^1
            and Milnor sequences
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lim%5E1+and+Milnor+sequences"} lim^1 and Milnor sequences
          %h3#descent_objects Descent objects
          %p
            %em Descent objects
            as they appear in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent and codescent
            are naturally conceived as homotopy limits. See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-stack"} infinity-stack
          %h3#SimpSheaves Homotopy (co)limits of simplicial pre(sheaves)
          %p
            The local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SPSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi proj
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi inj
                    %mi loc
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SPSh(C)_{proj/inj}^{loc}
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            serve as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models+for+%E2%88%9E-stack+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} models for ∞-stack (∞,1)-toposes
          %p Here we discuss some properties of homotopy limits and colimits in such model categories of simplicial presheaves.
          %h4#preservation_of_homotopy_pullback_by_inverse_images Preservation of homotopy pullback by inverse images
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C, C'
            two
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ←
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : Sh(C) \stackrel{\leftarrow}{\to} Sh(C')
            of
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} sheaf topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi SSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ←
                  %mi SSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p : SSh(C) \stackrel{\leftarrow}{\to} SSh(C') : p^*
          %p
            of simplicial (pre)sheaves. One would like this to extend to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            that recalls the fact that it came from a geometric morphism by the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi SSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SSh(C') \to SSh(C)
            preserves finite homotopy limits.
          %p
            In particular, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            have the same underlying category but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            the trivial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            in question is the inclusion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            which typically induces a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization"} Bousfield localization
            of model categories that models the injection of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-subcategory"} reflective (∞,1)-subcategory
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            = succeed "-presheaves." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SPSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi SPSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SPSh(C') \to SPSh(C)
            is
            = succeed "-stackification" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p The following result says that a strong version of this statement is true, at least for the preservation of homotopy pullbacks.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : Sh(C) \to Sh(C')
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
              of
              = succeed "es." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^* : Sh(C') \to Sh(C)
              be the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
              functor and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi SSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi SSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s p^* : SSh(C') \to SSh(C)
              be its degreewise extension to functor of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaf"} simplicial sheaf
              categories.
            %p
              Regarded as a functor between the corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+model+structure+on+simplicial+sheaves"} local injective model structures on simplicial sheaves
              on both sides
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                    %mo :
                    %mi SSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi inj
                      %mi loc
                    %mo →
                    %mi SSh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi inj
                      %mi loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    s p^* : SSh(C')_{inj}^{loc} \to SSh(C)_{inj}^{loc}
            %p this functor preserves homotopy pullbacks.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_6 Proof
            %p This appears as theorem 1.5 in
            %ul
              %li
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
                ,
                %em Fibrations and homotopy colimits of simplicial sheaves
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9811038"}> arXiv
                )
          %h3#more_examples_and_special_cases More examples and special cases
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+homotopy+colimit"} filtered homotopy colimit
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limit"} (∞,1)-limit
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimit"} lax (∞,1)-colimit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"}> mapping cone
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+telescope"} mapping telescope
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general_2 General
          %p Classical references:
          %ul
            %li#BousfieldKan
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aldridge+K.+Bousfield"} Aldridge K. Bousfield
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"} Daniel M. Kan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Limits%2C+Completions+and+Localizations"} Homotopy Limits, Completions and Localizations
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-540-38117-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-540-38117-4
            %li#Vogt73
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rainer+Vogt"}> Rainer Vogt
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy limits and colimits
                %strong 134
                (1973) 11-52 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://eudml.org/doc/171965"}> eudml:171965
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01219090"} doi:10.1007/BF01219090
          %p See
          %ul
            %li#Adams74
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Adams"}> Frank Adams
              , part III, section 8 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stable+homotopy+and+generalised+homology"} Stable homotopy and generalised homology
          %p More recently one has:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Dwyer"}> William Dwyer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Hirschhorn"} Philip Hirschhorn
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"} Daniel Kan
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeff+Smith"} Jeff Smith
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Limit+Functors+on+Model+Categories+and+Homotopical+Categories"} Homotopy Limit Functors on Model Categories and Homotopical Categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 113
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-113-s"} ISBN: 978-1-4704-1340-8
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dodo.pdmi.ras.ru/~topology/books/dhks.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
                , Chapter 5 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+Homotopy+Theory"} Categorical Homotopy Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107261457"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781107261457
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://emilyriehl.github.io/files/cathtpy.pdf"} pdf
          %p An introduction:
          %ul
            %li#Dugger
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Dugger"}> Daniel Dugger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A primer on homotopy colimits
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://people.math.rochester.edu/faculty/doug/otherpapers/hocolim.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www3.nd.edu/~stolz/2021S_Math80440/hocolim.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Dugger-PrimerHomotopyColimits.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p A general overview via universal properties is in the
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"} Georges Maltsiniotis
                lectures, Sevilla (2008)
              %p
                I, localizers, (
                %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Lecture_I_Localizers.pdf"}> pdf
                );
              %p
                II,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Lecture_II_Model_Categories.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                III,
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
                = succeed "):" do
                  %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Lecture_III_Derivators.pdf"} pdf
              %p
                IV, summary on derivators (
                %a{:href => "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~georges.maltsiniotis/Seville/Summary_on_Derivators.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p In
          %ul
            %li
              Wojciech Chacholski, Jerome Scherer,
              %em Homotopy theory of diagrams
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AT/0110316"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            is given a global definition of homotopy (co)limit as
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0110/0110316v1.pdf#page=19"} 4.1, p. 14
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0110/0110316v1.pdf#page=20"} 2.5, p. 15
          %p A nice discussion of the expression of homotopy colimits in terms of coends is in
          %ul
            %li#Gambino10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicola+Gambino"}> Nicola Gambino
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Weighted limits in simplicial homotopy theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.crm.cat/Publications/08/Pr790.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022404909002412"} publisher
          %p A collection of examples and exercises is in
          %ul
            %li#Isaacson
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Isaacson"}> Samuel Isaacson
              ,
              %em Excercises in homotopy colimits
              (
              %a{:href => "https://www3.nd.edu/~mbehren1/TAGS/Isaacson_exer.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Isaacson"}> Samuel Isaacson
              ,
              %em A note on unenriched homotopy coends
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.ma.utexas.edu/users/isaacson/PDFs/coends.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Homotopy limits for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"} triangulated categories
            are studied in
          %ul
            %li
              Marcel Bökstedt,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Amnon+Neeman"} Amnon Neeman
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopy limits in triangulated categories
              %strong 86
              (1993), no. 2, 209–234,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1214458"} MR94f:18008
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CM_1993__86_2_209_0"} numdam
          %p Other references are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Philip Hirschhorn,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Model categories and their localizations
            %li
              %p
                Dwyer, Hirschhorn, Kan, Smith,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Homotopy limit functors in model categories and homotopical categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"}> Michael Shulman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy limits and colimits and enriched homotopy theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610194"} math.CT/0610194
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kensuke+Arakawa"}> Kensuke Arakawa
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy Limits and Homotopy Colimits of Chain Complexes
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.00201"} arXiv:2310.00201
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergey+Arkhipov"}> Sergey Arkhipov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sebastian+%C3%98rsted"} Sebastian Ørsted
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopy (co)limits via homotopy (co)ends in general combinatorial model categories
                %strong 31
                6 (2023) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10485-023-09747-8"}> doi:10.1007/s10485-023-09747-8
                \&amp;rbrack; A version of
                %a{:href => "#Gambino10"} Weighted limits in simplicial homotopy theory
                that doesn’t require simplicial enrichment.
            %li
              %p
                Nicola Gambino,
                %em Homotopy limits for 2-categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.lacim.uqam.ca/~gambino/homotopy.pdf"}> pdf
                ), published as: Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society 145 (2008) 43-63.) Proves that homotopy limits in a 2-category with its natural model structure coincide with 2-categorical
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-limit"} pseudo-limits
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-limits"} 2-limits
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
                = succeed "-categories," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %li
              %p
                Andre Hirschowitz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"} Carlos Simpson
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AG/9807049"} Descent pour les n-champs
                = succeed "-categories." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,n)
            %li
              %p
                Beatriz Rodriguez Gonzalez,
                %em Realizable homotopy colimits
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1104.0646"}> arXiv:1104.0646
                )
            %li
              %p
                MathOverflow question:
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/38047/what-is-the-universal-problem-that-motivates-the-definition-of-homotopy-limits"} universal-problem-that-motivates-the-definition-of-homotopy-limits
          %p
            Discussion in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-Grothendieck+construction"} (infinity,1)-Grothendieck construction
            is in
          %ul
            %li#HeutsMoerdijk13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gijs+Heuts"}> Gijs Heuts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em Left fibrations and homotopy colimits
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1308.0704"}> arXiv:1308.0704
              )
          %p
            A discussion of homotopy limits via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limits"} weighted limits
            may be found in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dominique+Bourn"}> Dominique Bourn
              , and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Marc+Cordier"} Jean-Marc Cordier
              = succeed "," do
                %em A general formulation of homotopy limits
          %h3#in_homotopy_type_theory In homotopy type theory
          %p
            A formalization of some aspects of homotopy limits in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is
            = succeed "-coded" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Coq"} Coq
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guillaume+Brunerie"}> Guillaume Brunerie
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/guillaumebrunerie/HoTT/tree/master/Coq/Limits"} HoTT/Coq/Limits
            %li
              %p
                Jeremy Avigad,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Kapulkin"} Chris Kapulkin
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
                %em Homotopy limits in Coq
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1304.0680"}> arXiv:1304.0680
                )
